    "output_text": "```python\ndef edit_distance(s: str, t: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compute the edit distance (Levenshtein distance) between two strings\n    using dynamic programming.\n\n    The edit distance is the minimum number of operations (insert, delete, replace)\n    required to transform string s into string t.\n\n    Args:\n        s: The source string.\n        t: The target string.\n\n    Returns:\n        The minimum number of edit operations to convert s to t.\n\n    Time Complexity: O(m * n) where m = len(s) and n = len(t)\n    Space Complexity: O(m * n) for the 2D DP table\n    \"\"\"\n    m = len(s)\n    n = len(t)\n\n    # Create a 2D table (m+1) x (n+1)\n    # dp[i][j] = edit distance between s[0:i] and t[0:j]\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]\n\n    # Base cases:\n    # To convert s[0:i] to an empty string, we need i deletions\n    for i in range(m + 1):\n        dp[i][0] = i\n\n    # To convert an empty string to t[0:j], we need j insertions\n    for j in range(n + 1):\n        dp[0][j] = j\n\n    # Fill the table\n    for i in range(1, m + 1):\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            if s[i - 1] == t[j - 1]:\n                # No operation needed\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                # Take the minimum of:\n                # 1. Insert: dp[i][j-1] + 1\n                # 2. Delete: dp[i-1][j] + 1\n                # 3. Replace: dp[i-1][j-1] + 1\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + min(\n                    dp[i][j - 1],    # Insert\n                    dp[i - 1][j],    # Delete\n                    dp[i - 1][j - 1]  # Replace\n",
